event: response.output_text.done
data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_06ae9aae2904456d006a6cfe3b39cc8195a205f64fde480eea","logprobs":[],"output_index":4,"sequence_number":284,"text":"The latest notable news is that NASA’s Perseverance rover has completed the equivalent of a full marathon on Mars: 26.2 miles / 42.195 km. NASA says it reached that milestone on sol 1,890 of the mission, after about five years and four months of driving—much faster than Opportunity, which took over 11 years to cover the same distance. A Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter HiRISE image from June 13, 2026 shows Perseverance as a tiny speck near its tracks west of Jezero Crater in an area nicknamed “Arbot”【5:3†source】.\n\nPerseverance remains an active mission in Jezero Crater, where it is searching for signs of ancient microbial life and collecting rock/regolith cores for possible return to Earth by a future mission【5:4†source】. NASA’s mission page also highlights the rover’s scientifically important “Cheyava Falls” rock sample, nicknamed “Sapphire Canyon,” which contains potential biosignatures—clues that could be consistent with past life but require further study before any conclusion can be made【5:4†source】.\n\nIn short: the freshest update is a mobility milestone—Perseverance has “run a marathon” on Mars—while its broader science campaign continues to focus on ancient habitability, sample collection, and investigating possible biosignatures."}

